#import "/styles/lib.typ": *

= Rings and Ideals

== Rings and Ring Homomorphisms
#definition(title: "Ring")[
  A commutative ring with identity is a set $A$ with addition and multiplication such that

  + $(A,+)$ is an abelian group.
  + Multiplication is associative and commutative, with identity $1_A$.
  + Multiplication distributes over addition.
]
#remark[
  Throughout these notes, rings are commutative with identity, and ring homomorphisms preserve identity. The zero ring is allowed.
]
#example[
  + The matrix ring $M_n(bb(R))$ has the usual addition and multiplication. For $n >= 2$, its multiplication is noncommutative.
  + For a field $k$, the polynomial ring $k[x]$ consists of finite sums $sum_(i=0)^d a_i x^i$ with $a_i in k$ and $d >= 0$.
  + In $k[x_1, dots, x_n]$, the monomials are $x_1^(i_1) dots x_n^(i_n)$ with $(i_1, dots, i_n) in bb(Z)_(>= 0)^n$. Each polynomial has coefficients in $k$ and finite support.
]
#definition(title: "Homomorphism")[
  A map $phi : A -> B$ of rings is a homomorphism if it preserves addition, multiplication and identity.
]
#remark[
  The composite $g compose f : A -> C$ of ring homomorphisms $f : A -> B$ and $g : B -> C$ is a ring homomorphism.
]

== Ideals and Quotient Rings
#definition(title: "Ideal")[
  An ideal $frak(a)$ of $A$ is an additive subgroup of $A$ such that $a b in frak(a)$ for every $a in A$ and $b in frak(a)$.
]
#example[
  For a ring homomorphism $phi : A -> B$, its kernel $Ker(phi) = {a in A : phi(a)=0}$ is an ideal. It is an additive subgroup, and $phi(b a)=phi(b)phi(a)=0$ for $a in Ker(phi)$ and $b in A$.
]
#definition(title: "Quotient")[
  For an ideal $frak(a)$, the quotient ring $A\/frak(a)$ consists of the residue classes $a+frak(a)$, with addition and multiplication induced by those in $A$. Replacing either representative by one in the same class changes the sum and product by elements of $frak(a)$.
]
#remark[
  The natural map $pi : A -> A\/frak(a)$, $a |-> a+frak(a)$, is a surjective ring homomorphism with kernel $frak(a)$.
]
#proposition()[
  The ideals of $A$ containing $frak(a)$ correspond, in an inclusion-preserving bijection, to the ideals of $A\/frak(a)$. The maps are $frak(b) |-> frak(b)\/frak(a)$ and $frak(c) |-> pi^(-1)(frak(c))$.
]<prop:quotient-ideals>
#proof[
  Surjectivity of $pi$ makes $pi(frak(b))$ an ideal of the quotient, and gives $pi(pi^(-1)(frak(c)))=frak(c)$. Every inverse image is an ideal containing $Ker(pi)=frak(a)$. Conversely, if $x in pi^(-1)(pi(frak(b)))$, then $pi(x)=pi(y)$ for some $y in frak(b)$, so $x-y in Ker(pi)=frak(a) subset.eq frak(b)$ and hence $x in frak(b)$; the reverse inclusion is clear. Both operations preserve inclusion.
]

== Zero-divisors, Nilpotent Elements and Units
#definition(title: "Zero-divisor")[
  An element $x in A$ is a zero-divisor if $x y=0$ for some $y != 0$ in $A$. An integral domain is a nonzero ring in which the product of any two nonzero elements is nonzero.
]
#definition(title: "Nilpotent")[
  An element $x in A$ is nilpotent if $x^n=0$ for some integer $n >= 1$.
]
#remark[
  In a nonzero ring, each nilpotent is a zero-divisor. For $x=0$ take $y=1$. For a nonzero nilpotent $x$, take $y=x^(n-1)$ where $n$ is the least positive exponent with $x^n=0$.
]
#definition(title: "Unit")[
  An element $x in A$ is a unit if $x y=1_A$ for some $y in A$.
]
#remark[
  The inverse $y=x^(-1)$ is unique, and the units of $A$ form a multiplicative abelian group.
]
#definition(title: "Principal")[
  An ideal is principal if it is generated by one element, that is, it has the form $(a)={a b : b in A}$ for some $a in A$.
]
#definition(title: "Principal ideal domain")[
  A principal ideal domain is an integral domain in which every ideal is principal.
]
#definition(title: "Field")[
  A field is a nonzero ring whose only nonunit is zero.
]
#proposition()[
  For a nonzero ring $A$, the following are equivalent.

  1. $A$ is a field.
  2. The only ideals of $A$ are $(0)$ and $(1)$.
  3. Every ring homomorphism $A -> B$ to a nonzero ring $B$ is injective.
]
#proof[
  $(1) => (2)$. A nonzero ideal contains a nonzero element and hence a unit, so it contains $1$.

  $(2) => (3)$. The kernel is proper since $1_B != 0$, so it is zero.

  $(3) => (1)$. If $x$ is a nonunit then $(x)$ is proper and $A\/(x)$ is nonzero. Injectivity of the natural map $A -> A\/(x)$ gives $(x)=(0)$, hence $x=0$.
]

== Prime Ideals and Maximal Ideals
#definition(title: "Prime ideal")[
  A prime ideal $frak(p)$ is a proper ideal such that $x y in frak(p)$ implies $x in frak(p)$ or $y in frak(p)$.
]
#definition(title: "Maximal ideal")[
  A maximal ideal $frak(m)$ is maximal under inclusion among the proper ideals of $A$.
]
#remark[
  The definition of prime translates to $A\/frak(p)$ being an integral domain. By @prop:quotient-ideals and the field criterion, $frak(m)$ is maximal exactly when $A\/frak(m)$ is a field. Since fields are domains, every maximal ideal is prime. The ideal $(0)$ is prime exactly when $A$ is a domain.
]
#theorem()[
  Every nonzero ring has a maximal ideal.
]<thm:maximal-ideal>
#proof[
  The set of proper ideals is nonempty since it contains $(0)$. The union of a nonempty chain of proper ideals is an ideal because any two of its elements belong to a common ideal in the chain. It remains proper since membership of $1$ would put $1$ in one of the ideals. Thus Zorn's lemma gives a maximal element.
]
#corollary()[
  Every proper ideal $frak(a)$ is contained in a maximal ideal of $A$.
]
#proof[
  Apply @thm:maximal-ideal to $A\/frak(a)$ and take the inverse image under the quotient map, using @prop:quotient-ideals.
]
#corollary()[
  Every nonunit of $A$ belongs to some maximal ideal, and every element of a maximal ideal is a nonunit.
]<non-unit>
#proof[
  The principal ideal generated by a nonunit is proper, so the preceding corollary applies. An ideal containing a unit is the whole ring.
]
#definition(title: "Local ring")[
  A local ring is a ring with a unique maximal ideal $frak(m)$. The field $A\/frak(m)$ is the residue field of $A$.
]
#proposition()[
  1. If $frak(m)$ is a proper ideal and every element of $A without frak(m)$ is a unit, then $A$ is local with maximal ideal $frak(m)$.
  2. If $frak(m)$ is maximal and $1+x$ is a unit for every $x in frak(m)$, then $A$ is local.
]
#proof[
  Under (1), every proper ideal lies in $frak(m)$ since a proper ideal consists of nonunits. Under (2), for $a in A without frak(m)$ we have $(a)+frak(m)=A$. Write $a b=1+x$ with $x in frak(m)$. The product $a b$ is a unit, so $a$ is a unit and (1) applies.
]
#example[
  + In $A = bb(Z)$ every ideal is $(m)$ for some $m >= 0$. The ideal $(m)$ is prime exactly when $m=0$ or $m$ is a prime number, and the ideals $(p)$ with $p$ prime are maximal.
  + In $A = k[x_1, dots, x_n]$ the ideal $frak(m)$ of polynomials with zero constant term is maximal, being the kernel of $f |-> f(0)$. For $n >= 2$ it is not principal: if $frak(m)=(f)$, then $f$ divides both $x_1$ and $x_2$; these have no common divisor other than units, so $f$ is a unit, forcing $frak(m)=(1)$.
  + In a principal ideal domain every nonzero prime ideal is maximal. If $(x) != 0$ is prime and $(x) ⊊ (y)$, write $x = y z$ with $y in.not (x)$. Since $(x)$ is prime, $z in (x)$, say $z=t x$; then $x=y t x$ and $y t=1$.
]

== Nilradical and Jacobson Radical
#proposition()[
  The nilpotent elements of $A$ form an ideal $frak(N)$, and the only nilpotent of $A\/frak(N)$ is zero.
]
#proof[
  If $x^m=0$ and $y^n=0$, then every term in the binomial expansion of $(x+y)^(m+n-1)$ vanishes. Also $(a x)^m=0$ for every $a in A$. Thus the nilpotents form an ideal. If $(x+frak(N))^m=0$, then $x^m in frak(N)$, so $(x^m)^n=0$ for some $n$ and $x+frak(N)=0$.
]
#definition(title: "Nilradical")[
  The ideal $frak(N)$ of nilpotent elements is the nilradical of $A$.
]
#proposition()[
  The nilradical is the intersection of all prime ideals of $A$. An empty intersection of ideals is understood to be $A$.
]<prop:radical-form>
#proof[
  Every prime ideal contains each nilpotent, by repeated application of primality. For the reverse inclusion take a non-nilpotent $x$ and put $S={1,x,x^2,dots}$. The zero ideal is disjoint from $S$, and the union of any nonempty chain of ideals disjoint from $S$ remains an ideal disjoint from $S$. By Zorn's lemma there is an ideal $frak(p)$ maximal among those disjoint from $S$.

  If $a,b in A without frak(p)$, both $frak(p)+(a)$ and $frak(p)+(b)$ meet $S$. Write $x^m=u+r a$ and $x^n=v+s b$ with $u,v in frak(p)$ and $r,s in A$. If $a b in frak(p)$, multiplying gives $x^(m+n) in frak(p)$, contrary to disjointness. Thus $frak(p)$ is prime and excludes $x$.
]
#definition(title: "Jacobson radical")[
  The Jacobson radical $frak(R)$ of $A$ is the intersection of all maximal ideals of $A$.
]
#proposition()[
  An element $x in A$ belongs to $frak(R)$ exactly when $1-x y$ is a unit for every $y in A$.
]<prop:jacobson-units>
#proof[
  If $x in frak(R)$ and $1-x y$ is a nonunit, then @non-unit puts $1-x y$ in a maximal ideal $frak(m)$. Since $x y in frak(m)$, their sum $1$ belongs to $frak(m)$, a contradiction.

  Conversely, if a maximal ideal $frak(m)$ excludes $x$, then $(x)+frak(m)=A$. Write $1=x y+z$ with $z in frak(m)$. The element $1-x y=z$ is a nonunit, giving the converse.
]
#remark[
  Since maximal ideals are prime, the intersection defining the nilradical is contained in the Jacobson radical. The nilradical records which elements are nilpotent. The Jacobson radical records which elements $x$ make every $1-x y$ a unit, by @prop:jacobson-units.
]
#example[
  In $A=bb(Z)\/12 bb(Z)$, the units are the classes of $1,5,7,11$. The nonunits form $(overline(2)) union (overline(3))$ and are zero-divisors, since $overline(6)$ kills the first ideal and $overline(4)$ the second. By @prop:quotient-ideals, each ideal of $A$ is $(overline(d))$ for a positive divisor $d$ of $12$, and reduction modulo $d$ identifies $A\/(overline(d))$ with $bb(Z)\/d bb(Z)$. This quotient is a domain exactly for $d=2,3$, when it is also a field. Thus both radicals are $(overline(2)) inter (overline(3))={overline(0),overline(6)}$. The nonunit $overline(2)$ lies in the first maximal ideal and outside the second, so it is a non-nilpotent zero-divisor.
]

== Operations on Ideals
=== Sums, Intersections and Products
The sum $frak(a)+frak(b)={x+y : x in frak(a), y in frak(b)}$ is the smallest ideal containing both. For a family $(frak(a)_i)_(i in I)$ the sum consists of all finite sums $sum_i x_i$ with $x_i in frak(a)_i$.

The intersection $frak(a) inter frak(b)$ consists of the elements common to both ideals. Arbitrary intersections of ideals are ideals, so the ideals of $A$ form a complete lattice.

The product $frak(a)frak(b)$ is the ideal generated by the products $x y$ with $x in frak(a)$ and $y in frak(b)$; it consists of all finite sums $sum_i x_i y_i$. The product of a finite family is defined similarly, so the powers $frak(a)^n$ for $n >= 1$ are defined, with $frak(a)^0=(1)$.

#proposition()[
  For ideals $frak(a),frak(b),frak(c)$ of $A$,
  $
    frak(a)(frak(b)+frak(c)) = frak(a)frak(b)+frak(a)frak(c).
  $
]<prop:distributive>
#proof[
  Expanding each summand $a_i(b_i+c_i)$ gives the forward inclusion. The ideals $frak(b)$ and $frak(c)$ lie in $frak(b)+frak(c)$, which gives the reverse inclusion.
]
#remark[
  Sum, intersection and product are each commutative and associative, and the product distributes over the sum. Sum and intersection do not distribute over each other in general: the best general statement is the modular law $frak(a) inter (frak(b)+frak(c))=frak(a) inter frak(b)+frak(a) inter frak(c)$ when $frak(a) ⊇ frak(b)$ or $frak(a) ⊇ frak(c)$. Always $frak(a)frak(b) subset.eq frak(a) inter frak(b)$. For ideals of $bb(Z)$ both distributive laws hold, because ideals are generated by gcd and lcm.
]
=== Coprime Ideals
#definition(title: "Coprime")[
  Ideals $frak(a),frak(b)$ are coprime if $frak(a)+frak(b)=A$, equivalently if $u+v=1$ for some $u in frak(a)$ and $v in frak(b)$.
]
#remark[
  For coprime ideals, $frak(a) inter frak(b)=frak(a)frak(b)$. The product lies in both ideals. Conversely, choose $u in frak(a)$ and $v in frak(b)$ with $u+v=1$. For $z in frak(a) inter frak(b)$, both summands in $z=z u+z v$ belong to the product.
]
=== Direct Products and the Chinese Remainder Theorem
For rings $A_1,dots,A_n$, the ring $product_(i=1)^n A_i$ has componentwise addition and multiplication. Each coordinate projection onto $A_i$ is a surjective homomorphism.

#proposition()[
  Let $frak(a)_1,dots,frak(a)_n$ be ideals of $A$ with $n >= 1$. The natural map
  $
    phi : A -> product_(i=1)^n (A\/frak(a)_i),
    quad x |-> (x+frak(a)_i)_(i=1)^n
  $
  has kernel $inter.big_(i=1)^n frak(a)_i$. It is surjective exactly when the ideals are pairwise coprime. In that case $inter.big_(i=1)^n frak(a)_i=product_(i=1)^n frak(a)_i$.
]<prop:crt>
#proof[
  A tuple $phi(x)$ vanishes exactly when $x$ lies in every $frak(a)_i$. If $phi$ is surjective, lift the tuple with coordinate $1$ at $i$ and zero elsewhere. The lift $e_i$ satisfies $1-e_i in frak(a)_i$ and $e_i in frak(a)_j$ for $j != i$, so the pair is coprime.

  Conversely, for each $j != i$ choose $u_(i j) in frak(a)_j$ congruent to $1$ modulo $frak(a)_i$. Then $e_i=product_(j != i) u_(i j)$ has the same residues and lies in the product of the ideals other than $frak(a)_i$. The tuple $(x_i+frak(a)_i)_i$ is the image of $sum_i x_i e_i$. Also $1-e_n in frak(a)_n$, so the product of the first $n-1$ ideals is coprime to $frak(a)_n$ for $n >= 2$. Induction using the two-ideal case gives the intersection formula.
]
#remark[
  For a finite pairwise coprime family, @prop:crt gives $Ker(phi)=product_i frak(a)_i$. The map on residue classes $x+Ker(phi) |-> phi(x)$ is a well-defined injective ring homomorphism, and surjectivity of $phi$ makes it an isomorphism $A\/(product_i frak(a)_i) tilde.equiv product_i (A\/frak(a)_i)$. In particular the coprime ideals $(4)$ and $(3)$ of $bb(Z)$ give the splitting of $bb(Z)\/12 bb(Z)$ by reduction modulo $4$ and $3$.
]

=== Unions of Ideals
The union of two ideals is an ideal exactly when one contains the other. Indeed, elements taken from the two set differences have a sum outside both ideals.

#proposition()[
  1. If an ideal $frak(a)$ is contained in a finite union of prime ideals $frak(p)_1,dots,frak(p)_n$, then $frak(a) subset.eq frak(p)_i$ for some $i$.
  2. If a prime ideal $frak(p)$ contains a finite intersection of ideals $frak(a)_1,dots,frak(a)_n$, then $frak(a)_i subset.eq frak(p)$ for some $i$. If moreover $frak(p)=inter.big_i frak(a)_i$, then $frak(p)=frak(a)_i$ for some $i$.
]<prop:1.11>
#proof[
  For (1), discard primes contained in another listed prime. Suppose each remaining prime excludes some $a_i in frak(a)$. For $j != i$, take $b_(i j) in frak(p)_j without frak(p)_i$. Then $c_i=a_i product_(j != i)b_(i j)$ lies in $frak(a)$ and in all the listed primes except $frak(p)_i$, by primality. The sum $sum_i c_i$ lies outside every listed prime, a contradiction.

  For (2), if each $frak(a)_i$ has an element $a_i$ outside $frak(p)$, then $product_i a_i$ belongs to the intersection and lies outside $frak(p)$, again by primality. If equality holds, then $frak(p) subset.eq frak(a)_i$ for the index just found, and the reverse inclusion is the hypothesis, so $frak(p)=frak(a)_i$.
]

=== Ideal Quotients and Annihilators
For ideals $frak(a),frak(b)$ of $A$, the ideal quotient is $(frak(a):frak(b))={x in A : x frak(b) subset.eq frak(a)}$, and the annihilator of $frak(b)$ is $Ann(frak(b))=(0:frak(b))$. For $x in A$, write $(frak(a):x)=(frak(a):(x))$ and $Ann(x)=(0:x)$. The set of zero-divisors is $D=union.big_(x != 0) Ann(x)$.

=== Radicals
The radical of an ideal is $r(frak(a))={x in A : x^n in frak(a) "for some" n >= 1}$.

#proposition()[
  The radical $r(frak(a))$ is the intersection of all prime ideals containing $frak(a)$.
]
#proof[
  Under the quotient map $pi : A -> A\/frak(a)$, the inverse image of the nilradical of $A\/frak(a)$ is $r(frak(a))$. Prime ideals of the quotient correspond to primes containing $frak(a)$ by @prop:quotient-ideals and the prime ideal criterion. Take inverse images in @prop:radical-form.
]
#proposition()[
  If $D$ is the set of zero-divisors of $A$, then $D=union.big_(x != 0) r(Ann(x))$.
]<prop:1.15>
#proof[
  The inclusion from $D$ follows from $Ann(x) subset.eq r(Ann(x))$. For the converse, if $x != 0$ and $a^n x=0$, choose the least such $n >= 1$. Then $a^(n-1)x != 0$ is annihilated by $a$, so $a in D$.
]
#proposition()[
  If $r(frak(a))+r(frak(b))=A$, then $frak(a)+frak(b)=A$.
]
#proof[
  If the sum were proper, a maximal ideal containing it would contain both radicals by primality, contradicting their sum being $A$.
]
=== Worked Examples
#example[
  In $A=bb(Z)$ take $frak(a)=(m)$ and $frak(b)=(n)$ with $m,n > 0$.

  + $frak(a)+frak(b)=(gcd(m,n))$, while $frak(a) inter frak(b)=(lcm(m,n))$.
  + $frak(a)frak(b)=(m n)$, so $frak(a)frak(b)=frak(a) inter frak(b)$ exactly when $m$ and $n$ are coprime.
  + $r((m))=(p_1 dots p_r)$, where $p_1,dots,p_r$ are the distinct prime divisors of $m$. Thus $r((m))=(m)$ exactly when $m$ is squarefree.

  In $k[x_1,dots,x_n]$, the $s$-th power of $(x_1,dots,x_n)$ consists of the polynomials with no terms of degree $< s$.
]

== Extension and Contraction
For a ring homomorphism $f : A -> B$, the contraction of an ideal $frak(b)$ of $B$ is $frak(b)^c=f^(-1)(frak(b))$. The extension $frak(a)^e$ of an ideal $frak(a)$ of $A$ is the ideal of $B$ generated by $f(frak(a))$. An ideal of $A$ is called contracted if it is the contraction of an ideal of $B$. An ideal of $B$ is called extended if it is the extension of an ideal of $A$.
#proposition()[
  1. $frak(a) subset.eq frak(a)^(e c)$ and $frak(b)^(c e) subset.eq frak(b)$.
  2. $frak(a)^e=frak(a)^(e c e)$ and $frak(b)^c=frak(b)^(c e c)$.
  3. Extension and contraction restrict to inverse bijections between the contracted ideals of $A$ and the extended ideals of $B$.
]<prop:extension-contraction>
#proof[
  By definition, $f(frak(a)) subset.eq frak(a)^e$ and $f(frak(b)^c) subset.eq frak(b)$. Taking inverse images and generated ideals, respectively, gives (1). Both operations preserve inclusion. Extending the first inclusion in (1) and applying the second to $frak(a)^e$ gives the first equality in (2). Contracting the second inclusion in (1) and applying the first to $frak(b)^c$ gives the other equality.

  By (2), a contracted ideal $frak(a)=frak(b)^c$ satisfies $frak(a)^(e c)=frak(a)$, and an extended ideal $frak(b)=frak(a)^e$ satisfies $frak(b)^(c e)=frak(b)$. Each equality also exhibits the ideal as a contraction or extension, respectively. Thus on these two sets of ideals the two composites are identities.
]